  "note": "An Inf Div in the mountains with a Bde on each of two valleys or terrain compartments and the reserves split to guard them: \"If fighting in mountains, the divisional reserves may have to be split to guard approaches along different valleys or terrain compartments\" (GSP 1947 Inf Div in Battle s27 para 4 p 122); an immediate counter-attack has the greater chance, so the reserves are split and positioned to launch at once and concentrate quickly (s39 para 11 p 176); upwards of Bn level the reserves cannot act from one central location (MW s12 p 169). The depth Bde gives a reserve group behind each forward Bde: the Bde less a Bn on the main approach (Lt), a Bn on the other. No Armd Regt in the reserve and no transport from the Div pool: armour is confined to roads and tracks (MW p 238); the reserves move on foot. No Security Zone: along the LoC in Kashmir and the Northern Areas none exists, and the En softens up selected posts (MW p 164). Minefields thickest in the valleys and foothills (MW p 150). The reserve groups occupy the counter-penetration posns behind their valleys as the threat warrants (GSP s27 para 5 b (5) p 123). The Bdes' own layouts are built as Bde plans (Bde Def (Mtn) for a salient).",
